Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fluid: a stand-in is a phase like any other, decided by
 the vessel's own split (form 4, i)

Physics. Liquid CO2 let down into a tank of its gas at 1 atm freezes in part at once: dry ice and vapour at the
sublimation point, the frost's latent heat keeping the gas warm. Until the vessel's next refresh the frost's share is
carried condensed (the stand-in), so a vessel that receives such a stream must gain it like any other phase, and the
vessel's own balance decides whether it stands. In a supersaturated gas whose CO2 has no liquid to stand in (CO2 in a
nitrogen or methane tank) the stand-in re-evaporates, and the vessel keeps its gas.

What the code did. D31 B took the stand-in's vote away in every node: a TP answer's stand-in was never proposed. That
was the only way a vessel already holding something gained the frost's condensed carrier (arrivingPhases gives one to
a junction, and under D31 A to an empty bag). In M3's let-down gate (liquid CO2 at 240 K and 2 MPa into a tank of CO2
gas at 1 atm) the vapour carried the frost's latent deficit, fell below CO2's 90 K floor, and the step held
(thermo-domain, 237 times); the owner's test world met the same hold (F1).

What it does now. phaseCheck proposes what a node lacks (lacking): a liquid of its own where it holds none of its own,
and a stand-in where it holds no liquid at all. The own split decides it under the D26 amendment. The let-down keeps
its stand-in (184.81 K, 2.2e-4 m3, then 0.200 kg of dry ice at 194.63 K at the refresh, as before D31 B). A frosted
nitrogen tank's stand-in is driven to nothing and refused for the solve. D31 B's completeness half stays: a stand-in
never makes a node liquid-complete, so a real drop is still proposed beside it. The frosted-tank gate now states that
its stand-in is decided by its own split and refused; with D31 B's proposal half back it fails (no own split runs),
as does the let-down gate.

Owner decision (2026-10-16), form 4.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
